// Gobra contracts for Service — F1, F2, F4.
//
// RETARGETED FOR THE FLAT RESHAPE (S-12). These contracts target the public
// service API; internal data-race freedom (F5, Go scope) is established at the
// store layer.
//
// # LockP() composition — unchanged in shape
//
// `LockP()` composes the per-component predicates of the verified primitives
// Service holds. `New()` folds it after constructing the underlying clock, id
// generators and store. The scope choice from the original design still
// stands: `clk` is a `clock.Clock` INTERFACE, and Gobra cannot fold the
// concrete `*clock.Logical.LockP()` predicate through an interface field, so
// the composition holds only `acc(&s.clk)` — field-existence permission, no
// predicate fold. That is why `tickLogical` and `nowLogical` in service.go are
// still `// @ trusted`: they quarantine the runtime type-assertion whose
// result Gobra cannot connect back to the concrete predicate. The flat reshape
// had no bearing on either of them — it changed the store's containers, not
// the clock's interface dispatch — and both remain trusted for exactly the
// reason they always were.
//
// # What actually changed here
//
// `GhostHomeTimeline` is RETIRED. It was a ghost stub declaring
//
//	func (s *Service) GhostHomeTimeline(user string, limit int) (result []Tweet)
//
// with an F2 ordering postcondition attached to it, and a comment saying
// "F1 + F2 are inherited from store.GhostHomeTimeline; service is a
// pass-through." Three things were wrong with it after S-03:
//
//  1. `store.GhostHomeTimeline` no longer exists — it was retired when the
//     real store method became its own proof.
//  2. The signature is stale: the real method took a `cursor int64` third
//     parameter and started returning `(out []dom.Tweet, more bool)`.
//  3. `Tweet` is not a type in this package; the element type is `dom.Tweet`.
//
// A ghost stub that names a retired function, with the wrong arity and an
// undefined element type, cannot discharge anything. Keeping it would have
// meant carrying an F2 claim on a declaration nothing checks — the exact
// shape of the unfalsifiable-check problem this repository exists to measure
// (see finding F001).
//
// It is retired rather than repaired because the REAL method now carries the
// property. `(*Service).HomeTimeline` in service.go has the F2 ordering clause
// as a genuine postcondition, forwarded verbatim from the store's, and Gobra
// discharges it against the real body. The proof moved from a stub to the
// code, which is the direction it should move.
//
// # F1 is still not proved, here or below
//
// The retired stub also claimed F1. It is not replaced, and this is not an
// oversight: the store cannot express the visibility property either (see the
// F1 section in memstore.gobra — it needs the returned elements related back
// to their source log positions). Service is a pass-through, so it can export
// no more than the store proves. F1 stays runtime-enforced and R0/R1/R2-
// covered; only F2 is discharged at R4.

package service

// LockP is the lock-invariant predicate for *Service. It composes the
// component LockP() predicates of the verified primitives Service holds, plus
// field-existence permission for the interface-typed `clk`.
pred (s *Service) LockP() {
	acc(&s.clk) &&
	acc(&s.idsTw) && acc(s.idsTw.LockP()) &&
	acc(&s.idsUsr) && acc(s.idsUsr.LockP()) &&
	acc(&s.st) && acc(s.st.LockP())
}

// Stream 3 Phase 5 sub-PR 2: `GhostPostTweet` retired — `(*Service).PostTweet`
// is now its own proof in service.go (framing-only postcondition). The
// success-path payload assertions (`t.Author == author`, `t.Text == text`,
// `t.ID >= 1`) would require composing the Service-level `LockP()` with the
// inner `MemStore.LockP()` `unfolding`-clauses, which the store's predicate
// does not expose. Both stay runtime-enforced by the explicit field
// assignments in the `dom.Tweet{...}` literal and by the discharged
// `(*MemStore).PutTweet` body.
